<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Four theories of loneliness tested against a sample of interviews</title><!--[if gte mso 9]><xml><w:WordDocument><w:View>Print</w:View></w:WordDocument></xml><![endif]--><style>body,p,li,td{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.35;color:#17130f}h1,h2,h3{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-weight:normal;color:#8c1912}h1{font-size:20pt}h2{font-size:14pt}p.note{font-size:9pt}td,th{border:1px solid #e4dcd0;padding:3pt 5pt;font-size:9pt;vertical-align:top}table{border-collapse:collapse}</style></head><body><p style="color:#8b7f72;font-size:9pt">RUBICON REPORT</p><h1>In a random sample of 12 interviews (3 per borough, seeded draw), how far do young people&#x27;s first-hand accounts of loneliness support or contradict four theories: discrepancy (quality not quantity of ties), Weiss&#x27;s emotional versus social loneliness, Cacioppo&#x27;s self-reinforcing withdrawal loop, and social media as displacing versus compensating for face-to-face contact?</h1><p>Of the four theories, discrepancy theory fits these accounts best: 9 of 12 interviews support it outright, 2 of 12 show it alongside plain absence of people, and 1 of 12 contradicts it. The other three theories find support in a minority of interviews and are silent in many, so the sample can say little for or against them. The sample is 12 interviews drawn at random, three from each borough, and every count below rests only on what respondents said about their own lives.</p><h2>Discrepancy: quality of ties, not their number</h2>
<p>Most respondents describe being lonely with people around them. One felt far from family he lived with: &quot;Even though I see them, probably like, 23 hours in a day, I just feel like I was so far away from everyone&quot; <sup>1</sup>. Another had company for nights out but not for hard times: &quot;I was around people but just not around the right people&quot; <sup>2</sup>. Others speak of &quot;temporary friends, as opposed to real friends&quot; <sup>3</sup> and of not being able to talk to friends &quot;because you feel like they might not understand you&quot; <sup>4</sup>.</p>
<p>The one contradicting account puts loneliness down to an empty room: &quot;Just someone sitting on another bed or… you won’t feel lonely&quot; <sup>5</sup>. The two mixed accounts add a lonely spell after a house move, &quot;because I didn&#x27;t have anyone there&quot; <sup>6</sup>, and night-time thoughts that come &quot;purely because no one is around&quot; <sup>7</sup>. So plain absence of people also matters, but in this sample it is the lesser story.</p>
<h2>Weiss: emotional against social loneliness</h2>
<p>2 of 12 interviews support Weiss and 2 of 12 show both support and contradiction; 0 of 12 contradict him outright, and 8 of 12 give no evidence either way. In support, one respondent felt close to his family but lonely at college, where &quot;you’re surrounded by people but you don’t know any of them really&quot; <sup>8</sup>. The mixed cases are telling: one man missed &quot;just one person you can talk to&quot; while he had friends and family, then describes his girlfriend filling the gap when friends were busy <sup>9</sup>; one woman lacked a confidant among her &quot;party people&quot; yet also says &quot;You can have a family and don’t have friends, you’re still fine&quot; <sup>10</sup>. That second half cuts against Weiss&#x27;s claim that one kind of tie cannot stand in for the other.</p>
<h2>Cacioppo: the self-reinforcing loop</h2>
<p>2 of 12 interviews support the loop, 3 of 12 show both withdrawal and reaching out, 1 of 12 contradicts it, and 6 of 12 give no first-hand account of what the respondent did when lonely. The clearest support: &quot;I physically trapped myself in my room because I didn’t wanna communicate with people&quot;, which &quot;kinda prolonged the experience&quot; <sup>11</sup>; the same respondent later recovered &quot;through my best friend and football and then my mates&quot; <sup>11</sup>. Another left a park when others came in, and says &quot;you remove yourself then you feel lonely&quot; <sup>12</sup>. The contradicting respondent says loneliness &quot;wouldn’t last&quot; because she would &quot;make up with this person or speak my mind&quot; <sup>13</sup>. Several respondents describe the loop clearly but only as what &quot;you&quot; would do, which this coding does not count <sup>14</sup> <sup>15</sup>. Read as a whole, the accounts fit Cacioppo&#x27;s fuller model, in which loneliness pushes both ways, better than the loop alone.</p>
<h2>Social media: displacement against compensation</h2>
<p>The two hypotheses come out close: 4 of 12 interviews describe displacement, 3 of 12 compensation, 1 of 12 both, and 4 of 12 tie social media to nothing in their own loneliness. Displacement here is mostly comparison and exclusion rather than lost time: &quot;when you see pictures that you&#x27;re not involved in and it just generally seems fun, that&#x27;s when you start feeling alone&quot; <sup>16</sup>, and phones at the table that &quot;kind of takes that connection away&quot; <sup>17</sup>. Compensation is about reaching people who are far away or not there: relatives abroad <sup>18</sup>, or the phone at night as &quot;the only way I can stop that from happening&quot; <sup>19</sup>. The one respondent coded both names lying in bed on Instagram as his loneliest place but credits his phone with why he does not feel lonely elsewhere <sup>20</sup>.</p>
<h2>What this sample cannot show</h2>
<ul><li>The interview task asked what respondents associate with loneliness, which invites talk of misunderstanding and quality; the strong showing for discrepancy theory may partly reflect the questions asked.</li><li>A small random sample cannot give prevalence, and the theories with many no-evidence cases (Weiss, the loop) are untested here rather than refuted: the interviews were not designed to probe them.</li><li>One coder read each interview, with no second coder; the strict first-hand rule set several vivid general statements aside, and a broader rule would raise the support counts for the loop in particular.</li></ul><h2>How this was made</h2><h3>What was read</h3><p>All 48 documents, read in full: 24 male, 24 female.</p>
<h3>Coding: T1 discrepancy</h3><p class="small">one row for each document, read whole; 12 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>You are reading one interview with a young person (18 to 24) in East London about loneliness. Part 1 asks what they associate with loneliness; part 2 asks about places where they feel most connected and most lonely. Decide how the interview bears on one theory. Discrepancy theory (Perlman and Peplau): loneliness is a gap between the relationships a person wants and the ones they have, especially in their quality (being understood, depth, closeness), not simply their number. Count only the respondent&#x27;s own experience of loneliness or connection (first-hand: what happened to them, what they felt, what they did). A general view about young people or &#x27;people&#x27; with no tie to the respondent&#x27;s own life does not count and goes to no_evidence. Read the whole interview, both parts, before deciding. Quote the single passage that best carries the value, copied exactly; for no_evidence quote the passage that comes closest to the theory, or the respondent&#x27;s first answer if nothing comes close.</blockquote></details>
<h4>Discrepancy</h4><p>How the respondent&#x27;s first-hand account bears on this theory: Discrepancy theory (Perlman and Peplau): loneliness is a gap between the relationships a person wants and the ones they have, especially in their quality (being understood, depth, closeness), not simply their number.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Supports</th><td>The respondent describes feeling lonely while people were around them, or because their ties lacked understanding, depth or closeness, or because relationships fell short of what they wanted or expected.</td><td>Lonely among family, friends or a crowd; misunderstood; friendships felt shallow or one-sided; nobody to really talk to although people were there.</td><td>Lonely only because nobody was there at all, with no mention of quality or of a gap from what they wanted: that is contradicts.</td></tr><tr><th>Contradicts</th><td>The respondent puts their own loneliness down simply to having too few people or being physically alone, and describes being around people (any people) as enough to end it.</td><td>Lonely when home alone and fine as soon as anyone is around; loneliness gone once there were more people, with quality never raised.</td><td>Physical aloneness that they also say felt lonely because ties were not close: that is supports or both.</td></tr><tr><th>Both</th><td>The interview holds a first-hand passage of each kind: one loneliness described as a gap in quality, another as plain absence of people that any company ended.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>No evidence</th><td>No first-hand passage bears on whether quality or quantity of ties drives the respondent&#x27;s loneliness.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Coding: T2 weiss</h3><p class="small">one row for each document, read whole; 12 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>You are reading one interview with a young person (18 to 24) in East London about loneliness. Part 1 asks what they associate with loneliness; part 2 asks about places where they feel most connected and most lonely. Decide how the interview bears on one theory. Weiss&#x27;s two kinds of loneliness: emotional loneliness (lacking one close attachment figure, such as a partner, a confidant or a parent one is close to) and social loneliness (lacking a wider network or group of friends, peers or community) are distinct, and one kind of tie cannot make up for the lack of the other. Count only the respondent&#x27;s own experience of loneliness or connection (first-hand: what happened to them, what they felt, what they did). A general view about young people or &#x27;people&#x27; with no tie to the respondent&#x27;s own life does not count and goes to no_evidence. Read the whole interview, both parts, before deciding. Quote the single passage that best carries the value, copied exactly; for no_evidence quote the passage that comes closest to the theory, or the respondent&#x27;s first answer if nothing comes close.</blockquote></details>
<h4>Weiss</h4><p>How the respondent&#x27;s first-hand account bears on this theory: Weiss&#x27;s two kinds of loneliness: emotional loneliness (lacking one close attachment figure, such as a partner, a confidant or a parent one is close to) and social loneliness (lacking a wider network or group of friends, peers or community) are distinct, and one kind of tie cannot make up for the lack of the other.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Supports</th><td>The respondent has one kind of tie yet feels lonely for want of the other kind: for example, many friends but no one close to confide in or no partner; or a close family member or partner but no friendship group or community.</td><td>Surrounded by friends but no one to tell the real things; close to mum but no friends at college; partner but no social circle.</td><td>Lonely with no ties of either kind: that is no_evidence for this theory.</td></tr><tr><th>Contradicts</th><td>The respondent describes one kind of tie as fully making up for the lack of the other, so that they did not feel lonely.</td><td>No close friends but family fills that gap completely; one best friend and needs nobody else, not lonely.</td><td>One tie that helps a little while loneliness remains: that is supports.</td></tr><tr><th>Both</th><td>The interview holds a first-hand passage of each kind.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>No evidence</th><td>No first-hand passage sets one kind of tie against the other.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Coding: T3 loop</h3><p class="small">one row for each document, read whole; 12 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>You are reading one interview with a young person (18 to 24) in East London about loneliness. Part 1 asks what they associate with loneliness; part 2 asks about places where they feel most connected and most lonely. Decide how the interview bears on one theory. Cacioppo&#x27;s self-reinforcing loop: loneliness makes people wary of others, so they withdraw, distrust or avoid contact, and that deepens the loneliness. Count only the respondent&#x27;s own experience of loneliness or connection (first-hand: what happened to them, what they felt, what they did). A general view about young people or &#x27;people&#x27; with no tie to the respondent&#x27;s own life does not count and goes to no_evidence. Read the whole interview, both parts, before deciding. Quote the single passage that best carries the value, copied exactly; for no_evidence quote the passage that comes closest to the theory, or the respondent&#x27;s first answer if nothing comes close.</blockquote></details>
<h4>Loop</h4><p>How the respondent&#x27;s first-hand account bears on this theory: Cacioppo&#x27;s self-reinforcing loop: loneliness makes people wary of others, so they withdraw, distrust or avoid contact, and that deepens the loneliness.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Supports</th><td>The respondent describes loneliness leading them to withdraw, isolate, distrust others or avoid contact, and that withdrawal keeping or making them lonelier.</td><td>Felt alone so stayed in their room and stopped replying, which made it worse; after being let down stopped trusting people and ended up more alone.</td><td>Withdrawal for other reasons (busy, studying) with no tie to loneliness: no_evidence.</td></tr><tr><th>Contradicts</th><td>The respondent describes loneliness prompting them to reach out, join something or seek company, and that easing it.</td><td>Felt lonely so joined a club, messaged a friend or went to the mosque, and felt better.</td><td>Reaching out that failed and was followed by withdrawal: that is supports.</td></tr><tr><th>Both</th><td>The interview holds a first-hand passage of each kind, such as withdrawing at one time and reaching out at another.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>No evidence</th><td>No first-hand passage on what the respondent did in response to feeling lonely.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Coding: T4 social media</h3><p class="small">one row for each document, read whole; 12 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>You are reading one interview with a young person (18 to 24) in East London about loneliness. Part 1 asks what they associate with loneliness; part 2 asks about places where they feel most connected and most lonely. Decide how the interview bears on one theory. Social media and loneliness: the displacement hypothesis (online contact replaces or worsens face-to-face ties and so deepens loneliness, including through comparison) against the compensation hypothesis (online contact eases loneliness by keeping or making connections). Count only the respondent&#x27;s own experience of loneliness or connection (first-hand: what happened to them, what they felt, what they did). A general view about young people or &#x27;people&#x27; with no tie to the respondent&#x27;s own life does not count and goes to no_evidence. Read the whole interview, both parts, before deciding. Quote the single passage that best carries the value, copied exactly; for no_evidence quote the passage that comes closest to the theory, or the respondent&#x27;s first answer if nothing comes close.</blockquote></details>
<h4>Social media</h4><p>How the respondent&#x27;s first-hand account bears on this theory: Social media and loneliness: the displacement hypothesis (online contact replaces or worsens face-to-face ties and so deepens loneliness, including through comparison) against the compensation hypothesis (online contact eases loneliness by keeping or making connections).</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Displaces</th><td>The respondent describes social media or phones making them lonelier: replacing face-to-face contact, comparison with others&#x27; lives, feeling left out, or contact that felt empty.</td><td>Scrolling and seeing everyone out without me; we just text and never meet; on my phone all day and feel more alone.</td><td>A general complaint about &#x27;people nowadays&#x27; on phones with no tie to their own loneliness: no_evidence.</td></tr><tr><th>Compensates</th><td>The respondent describes social media, messaging or online groups easing their loneliness or keeping them connected.</td><td>Messaging friends when alone helps; an online group of people who went through the same thing; video calls with family abroad.</td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Both</th><td>The interview holds a first-hand passage of each kind.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>No evidence</th><td>Social media or online contact is not tied to the respondent&#x27;s own loneliness or connection.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>What was counted</h3><ul><li>Documents by discrepancy <span class="small">(tab_discrepancy)</span></li><li>Documents by weiss <span class="small">(tab_weiss)</span></li><li>Documents by loop <span class="small">(tab_loop)</span></li><li>Documents by social media <span class="small">(tab_social_media)</span></li></ul>
<h3>The checks code ran</h3><ul><li>48 of 48 quotations found word for word in their document.</li><li>16 numbers in the answer, every one counted by code; 0 written by hand.</li><li>0 citations to passages that do not exist; 0 quotations not in the passage they cite.</li></ul><h2>Passages cited</h2><p class="note"><sup>1</sup> I32: "They live with me but even then I feel like I was so far away from them. Even though I see them, probably like, 23 hours in a day, I just feel like I was so far away from everyone"</p><p class="note"><sup>2</sup> I45: "Like for example when I was in uni I was I was around people but just not around the right people i was just around people for going out I have like my party people when I wanna go out they’re there but when I&#x27;m going through my own stress when I need someone around me like like to talk things to, they were not there"</p><p class="note"><sup>3</sup> I10: "So it comes to contact with lots of new…new people all the time, and some … a lot of them are more so like temporary friends, as opposed to real friends"</p><p class="note"><sup>4</sup> I17: "sometimes you can’t actually talk to your friends about what’s going on in your life, because you feel like they might not understand you or they might judge you or they might talk about you or things like that"</p><p class="note"><sup>5</sup> I21: "Not necessarily share the bed, but even share the room. Just someone sitting on another bed or… you won’t feel lonely to the point of you can turn around and go so how was your day, what’s- what’s your plans for tomorrow. [Interviewer: mm] If you was by yourself, you couldn’t turn to someone and go how was your day."</p><p class="note"><sup>6</sup> I43: "And when we first moved in here, um, it was quite lonely because I just stayed at home most of the time being because I didn&#x27;t have anyone there. So you felt socially lonely."</p><p class="note"><sup>7</sup> I41: "I start to think very depressing thoughts like stuff like what am I gonna do when my mom dies? Stuff like that. I’m going to be alone, am I going to die alone, stuff like that and that’s purely because no one is around, you have no human contacts."</p><p class="note"><sup>8</sup> I41: "whereas say, college, school or education in box 2, you, once again, you’re surrounded by people but you don’t know any of them really, you don’t really know anyone, you may have known someone five years, they don’t know you, they don’t know where you’re from"</p><p class="note"><sup>9</sup> I43: "If, if I’d say if I didn’t have a girlfriend now and I’m how I’m now, like I had a job but I didn’t have a girlfriend, but all my friends wasn&#x27;t there. That loneliness is, is just gonna be floating around because I had a long week at work. I want to go out my friends, but they&#x27;re not here because they&#x27;re busy whereas now I have a girlfriend"</p><p class="note"><sup>10</sup> I45: "I have like my party people when I wanna go out they’re there but when I&#x27;m going through my own stress when I need someone around me like like to talk things to, they were not there"</p><p class="note"><sup>11</sup> I32: "I was just to myself, so I thought: ‘I need to start doing this again.’ And I slowly, like, through my best friend and football and then my mates, it all slowly came back"</p><p class="note"><sup>12</sup> I41: "so you leave because you don’t wanna be in the situation ‘oh, who&#x27;s the weirdo sitting on the kids swing?’ ‘who&#x27;s the weirdo in the kids park?’ yeah, so you remove yourself then you feel lonely but it’s better to feel lonely than sad"</p><p class="note"><sup>13</sup> I39: "there were moments of time where I felt lonely because of for example anything. It wouldn’t last because I feel like eventually make up with this person or speak my mind or etc."</p><p class="note"><sup>14</sup> I05: "maybe you think like there’s something wrong with you, if you&#x27;re not invited and you feel alone, maybe you start thinking you&#x27;re weird, so then you distance yourself even more and then become more alone"</p><p class="note"><sup>15</sup> I21: "Yeah when you’re depressed, you just don’t wanna talk to no one. You just wanna be yourself in your room or wherever your space is. Not necessarily doing anything, just quiet. And um… when people are around you, it’s not quiet, so you push them away. And that’s how loneliness comes about. You make yourself lonely- lonelier every day."</p><p class="note"><sup>16</sup> I06: "so when you… when you see pictures that you&#x27;re not involved in and it just generally seems fun, that&#x27;s when you start feeling alone because you&#x27;re not… with them, you’re not going to be in the background smiling or anything, it’s more you sitting down on your bed or something and you&#x27;re looking, tapping, seeing that loads of photos"</p><p class="note"><sup>17</sup> I43: "Sometimes it does get annoying in terms of like, right, all of us came out, we’re sitting in the restaurant eating and just, just talk all of us. Even myself I do that because everyone else is on it and like, it kind of takes that connection away"</p><p class="note"><sup>18</sup> I45: "Communicating with people. You have different ways of communicating to different people. My aunts and uncles are in Africa, I still communicate with them through there, do you understand what I&#x27;m saying? I can reach people quicker and easier, I don&#x27;t need to travel to talk to you."</p><p class="note"><sup>19</sup> I41: "the only way I can stop that from happening is by going on my phone, again contacts - with anything, just doing something to keep your mind active, it helps but it will never stop it."</p><p class="note"><sup>20</sup> I21: "I’m not- sitting in your bed, just on your phone watching YouTube or on Instagram, that’s not beneficial to you. Going out, seeing people, showing your face, that’s beneficial."</p></body></html>